## 5. Implementation Proposal for PunchPal

### 5.1 Two modes: Classic (teaching) and Dynamic (drilling)

The user picks Classic or Dynamic on the Home screen via a segmented control between the StreakCard and the WorkoutCard. The choice persists in Zustand+AsyncStorage. Existing users default to Classic (preserves behavior); new users default to Dynamic.

| | **Classic** | **Dynamic** |
|---|---|---|
| **Round start** | Bell + immediate combo at t=0, full description at t=4–5 (~10–12s of speech) | Bell + immediate combo at t=0, no intro speech |
| **Mid-round content** | Combo notation called once at start; Claude-decided form reminders distributed across the remaining ~155s | Combos rotate at fixed cadence by tier; no fillers, no reminders, no motivational lines |
| **Combo cadence within round** | Single anchor combo for the entire round (no rotation) | Beginner 25s / Intermediate 17s / Advanced 12s between combos |
| **End-of-round** | Bell at t=170 (last 10s warning), bell at t=180 (end) | Same |
| **Mental model** | "Like working through one combo with a teaching coach who corrects your form" | "Like pad work — coach calls combos, you execute, they call the next one" |

**Both modes use the same combo notation** with embedded defense/footwork scaled by tier (see §5.2 and §7). The difference is *delivery*, not *content*.

### 5.2 Combo content by tier

The 9-tier system (`punchpal_user_stats.next_level_progress` 0–100 within each of beginner/intermediate/advanced) drives combo complexity:

| Tier | Combo shape | Defense/footwork embedding |
|---|---|---|
| **Beginner (T1–T3)** | 2–3 punches, head only | **None.** Pure punches, no embedded moves. |
| **Intermediate (T4–T6)** | 3–4 punches, mixed body/head | **Light.** One slip, roll, or pivot embedded per combo on average. |
| **Advanced (T7–T9)** | 4–6 punches | **Heavy.** Multiple moves embedded per combo — slip + pivot + roll patterns chained with punches. |

Examples:

- T2 (mid beginner): `1-2-3` → `"one, two, three"` (pure punches)
- T5 (mid intermediate): `1-2-slip_right-3` → `"one, two, slip right, three"` (one defense embedded)
- T8 (mid advanced): `1-slip_left-2-pivot-3b-roll-4` → `"one, slip left, two, pivot, three to the body, roll, four"` (multiple moves embedded)

This means defense and footwork are *taught through the combo itself* rather than as side-instructions. A user hears "slip right" between two punches and learns by doing.

### 5.3 Round timeline — Classic mode example (Intermediate, Tier 5)

```
t=0s     bell.mp3 + "one, two, slip right, three"    ← anchor combo (~3s of speech)
t=5s     Speech.speak(description) — full form/setup explanation (~10s of speech)
            "Jab to find range, throw the cross with hip rotation, slip the
             counter, finish with the lead hook. Keep your chin tucked the
             whole time."
t=45s    Speech.speak(reminder_1)  — "rotate the hip on the cross"
t=95s    Speech.speak(reminder_2)  — "guard up after the slip"
t=140s   Speech.speak(reminder_3)  — "chin down"
t=170s   bell.mp3 (10s warning) + "ten seconds"
t=180s   bell.mp3 (end of round)
```

Claude generates the description and reminders together — they're consistent. The number of reminders (3 here) was Claude's call based on combo complexity (within the tier bound of 3–5 for intermediate).

### 5.4 Round timeline — Dynamic mode example (Intermediate, Tier 5)

```
t=0s     bell.mp3 + "one, two, slip right, three"   ← anchor (~3s)
t=17s    Speech.speak(variation_1)                  ← "one, two-b, slip right, three"
t=34s    Speech.speak(variation_2)                  ← "double jab, two, slip right, three"
t=51s    Speech.speak(variation_3)                  ← "one, two, pivot, three"
t=68s    Speech.speak(variation_4)                  ← "one, two, slip right, three, four"
t=85s    Speech.speak(variation_5)                  ← anchor reannouncement
t=102s   Speech.speak(variation_6)                  ← "one, two-b, slip right, three-b"
t=119s   Speech.speak(variation_7)                  ← "one, pivot, two, three"
t=136s   Speech.speak(variation_8)                  ← "one, two, slip right, three, four"
t=153s   Speech.speak(variation_9)                  ← anchor reannouncement
t=170s   bell.mp3 (10s warning) + "ten seconds"
t=180s   bell.mp3 (end of round)
```

Intermediate gets ~10 distinct combo calls in the round at ~17s cadence. Anchor reannouncements at ~85s and ~153s serve as anchor points so the round has a rhythm. Variations all share the embedded `slip_right` from the anchor — when the user advances combos within a round, the embedded moves stay consistent so the user isn't re-learning footwork every 17 seconds.

### 5.5 Speech expansion rules (both modes)

The `expandForSpeech()` function in `mobile/src/lib/combo-variations.ts` handles all notation tokens:

- Numbers: `1→"one"`, `2→"two"`, `3→"three"`, `4→"four"`, `5→"five"`, `6→"six"`
- Body suffix: `Nb → "N to the body"` (e.g., `2b → "two to the body"`)
- Defense tokens: `slip_left → "slip left"`, `slip_right → "slip right"`, `roll → "roll"`, `roll_left → "roll left"`, `roll_right → "roll right"`, `block → "block"`, `parry → "parry"`
- Footwork tokens: `pivot → "pivot"`, `pivot_left → "pivot left"`, `pivot_right → "pivot right"`, `step_back → "step back"`, `step_in → "step in"`, `shuffle_left → "shuffle left"`, `shuffle_right → "shuffle right"`
- Joined with `, ` between tokens for natural TTS pacing.

### 5.6 Form reminder content (Classic mode only)

Claude picks reminder content from a mix of three categories per combo. No fixed order — Claude picks what's most useful for the specific combo:

1. **Form cues:** "chin down," "elbow tight," "guard up," "breathe out," "rotate the hip"
2. **Punch-specific corrections:** "rotate the hip on the cross," "snap the jab," "elbow in on the hook," "drop into the uppercut"
3. **Tempo cues:** "snap, don't push," "fast hands," "settle, then explode"

Reminder content must vary across the round — Claude is instructed not to repeat the same cue twice within a round, and to diversify the *category* of cue across reminders when possible.

### 5.7 Reminder count by tier (Claude bounded)

| Tier | Reminder count range |
|---|---|
| **Beginner** | 2–3 |
| **Intermediate** | 3–5 |
| **Advanced** | 4–6 |

Bounds chosen to keep cumulative in-round speech (excluding the opening description) under ~12 seconds for beginner, ~15 for intermediate, ~18 for advanced — the "annoying threshold" past which users feel the app is talking over their execution.

Reminders are distributed roughly evenly across the round but Claude is allowed to bunch toward the second half (real coaches correct more in the back half once the student has had time to attempt the combo).

### 5.8 Rest periods (both modes)

- 60s default rest between 3-min rounds.
- Coach speaks at `rest+2s`: *"Good round. Take a breath."*
- At `rest+25s`: a short technique tip for the *upcoming* round, generated by Claude once and stored on the round.
- At `rest+50s`: *"Ten seconds, get ready."*
- At `rest+60s`: bell + next round begins.

### 5.9 Scheduler architecture

Single chained `setTimeout` against absolute wall-clock time. Every delay computed from `roundStartedAt`, never from the previous timeout firing. `Speech.stop()` called only at round start, not before every utterance. `onDone` NOT used to gate the next utterance. Separate 250ms `setInterval` ticker drives the visible countdown only.

Pause/resume: `Speech.stop()` on pause, record `pausedAt`. On resume, add `(Date.now() - pausedAt)` to `pausedAccumMs` and re-schedule from the current index against the adjusted clock.

### 5.10 Speech-engine specifics (Expo Speech)

- Rate: 0.95 on iOS, 0.88 on Android (unchanged from current).
- Pre-expand notation server-side into `expanded_speech` field on each combo — runtime expansion in the client races against re-renders.
- Background audio: declare `UIBackgroundModes: ["audio"]` in `app.json` so TTS continues with the screen locked.
- `AppState` listener recomputes the schedule on foreground return.

### 5.11 UI

HomeScreen gets a segmented control between StreakCard and WorkoutCard:

```
┌────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLASSIC          │      DYNAMIC       │  ← 36pt tall, full card width
└────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Classic — one combo with form coaching     ← when Classic active
   Dynamic — combos rotate, real pad work     ← when Dynamic active
```

Active segment: filled `#DC2626` (Boxing Red), white bold uppercase text. Inactive: transparent, muted text.

TimerScreen unchanged in layout — same anchor combo card, same play/pause button. Combo card animates (scale + red border pulse) at each TTS fire moment. No "Up next" card. No pre-warning cues.

### 5.12 Risks and mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Classic gets repetitive after several rounds drilling the same combo | Form reminders mix categories; rest-period technique tip changes each round; users who want variety pick Dynamic |
| Dynamic feels mechanical / not coach-like | Embedded defense/footwork makes each combo feel like a complete teaching moment; anchor reannouncements at ~50% and ~85% give the round a structure |
| Tier-mismatched users (beginner toggles to Advanced via Profile) get overwhelmed | Embedded moves only appear at the user's actual tier; Profile change re-renders next workout at new tier |
| Expo TTS can't keep up at advanced 12s cadence | Pre-expanded speech strings, no runtime parsing during round. 12s ≥ 4× typical 3s utterance. Tested separately. |
| User pauses mid-combo | `Speech.stop()` + `pausedAccumMs` accounting; resume re-anchors absolute schedule cleanly |
| OS backgrounds the timer | Audio session + `AppState` listener recomputes schedule on foreground |
| Schema migration for existing v1 workouts | `schema_version: 2` flag; legacy v1 workouts render in current single-combo behavior; v2 uses new round/callout/notation shape |

### 5.13 Migration plan

**Phase 1+2 (combined, ~3 weeks):** This document. Everything in §5, §6, §7. Ships Classic + Dynamic, embedded defense/footwork notation, full progression system, level-up + demotion logic, cap-state variety. Edge Function rewrite + client TimerScreen rewrite + Zustand store updates + database column additions.

**Phase 3 (1 week, post-launch):** Skip Combo button on TimerScreen, per-tier cadence A/B testing based on round-completion telemetry, optional Apple premium voice integration.

**Phase 4 (TBD):** Adaptive learning — track which combos the user skips most often, feed back into Claude's prompt as "user struggles with X, avoid in next workout." Combo-pattern rotation for cap-state variety (currently option 1 of cap-variety; option 3 if telemetry warrants).

